{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition","id":619900,"title":"Classic World of Darkness - IV Edition","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-08T03:15:28-07:00","id":619900,"title":"Classic World of Darkness - IV Edition","content":"So, with VtM IV Edition announced I was wondering what people hope for, in regards to drastic changes in the game world mechanics. I named the topic &quot;Classic World of Darkness IV&quot; since I hope for some streamlining among all games in the cWoD (and I'm confident it won't stop at VtM).<br /> <br /> So, for me, there are a couple of things.<br /> 1. Change the combat. I'd like to see a Defense score - like in nWoD - and possibly variable total health levels. Attack, possibly Dodge, Damage, Soak was to many rolls for one action in combat, a system that reduces this would be very much appreciated.<br /> 2. When the Merits from nWoD are brought over, I hope it is revamped. Foreign Languages and stuff like that, especially if it can be learned during play, should be a Background (if it isn't a knowledge anymore).<br /> 3. I hope the Morality from nWoD make it into VtM and possibly the cWoD as a whole.<br /> 4. I like alternating difficulties, but, the system should have caps. Any difficulty raise above 9 (due to circumstances) should reduce the dice pool, every difficulty lowered below 5 should give bonus dice. Difficulties below 5 or above 10 simply break the probabilities too much.<br /> <br /> That is, from the top of my head, things I'd like to see changed in a new version of the cWoD mechanics.<br /> Any one else who would want a drastic overhaul of the mechanics ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-08T05:25:06-07:00","id":619931,"title":"","content":"A lot of people in the Vampire sub-forum.<br /> <br /> That said, I go for those changes and a lot more, but I personally doubt it will happen anytime soon. Surely it WILL happen, because cWoD is still a gold mine.<br /> <br /> For once it would be nice if the &quot;neutral&quot; rules, the ones that supposedly govern every single entity in a given setting instead of the ones brought fort by the covered splat, where actually the same for everyone.<br /> <br /> Cross-overs shouldn't need so much tinkering with rules by the ST.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-08T05:52:57-07:00","id":619955,"title":"","content":"Well, VtM IV is announced.<br /> <br /> Actually, I was also hoping, that OPP would release a &quot;Rules&quot; book for a cWoD IV in concert with a VtM IV book, but I guess they fear that it would be perceived as too much nWoD. But, yes, a streamlining of all the basic rules, including multiple actions and additional actions (regardless if the stem from Celerity, Tome, Rage or Quicksilver) would be nice...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-08T08:56:57-07:00","id":620098,"title":"","content":"Given the existence of the translation guides, and the placement of Rose Bailey on the Vampire 4th team to make sure it doesn't overlap with the nWoD or how the nWoD works, those all seem highly unlikely to come to pass. Point 3 in particular is confusing because Morality was basically just lifted from Humanity, and it went unused in many cWoD games because it wasn't really a thematic concern for them, or because they measured degeneration on an increasing scale rather than decreasing (Torment and Angst are basically inverted parallels; KotE even introduced P'o as an inverse Humanity trait that could mess up your Virtue and Humanity rolls).<br /> <br /> So while we're almost definitely going to have problems like extra actions or difficulty levels addressed in some manner, we've also been told that V4 is definitely not going to be a port of V:tM to V:tR rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-08T10:12:59-07:00","id":620221,"title":"","content":"I'm just tired of the idea that to keep the &quot;feel&quot; of the cWoD we have to stick with crappy rules, or translate the games to nWoD. I don't want the same rules as nWoD, I just want good rules for a change. And nWoD DOES have good examples that can be used as inspiration, not just copied.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-09T02:01:28-07:00","id":621006,"title":"","content":"The big drastic rule's change I'd like to see the most?<br /> <br /> Dump the current rule of 1 and linked method of botching. It's annoying on multiple levels, frequently disruptive, and a complete missed opportunity from a design perspective.<br /> <br /> That said, the cWOD wouldn't feel the same if things went the way of the nWOD rules where botches are rather rare without player buy-in for bennies.<br /> <br /> I would love to see V4 use 1s to add in a twists to successful rolls without failures, and the possibility of turning failures into botches without changing the math. Even if it was something as simple as, &quot;if you roll more 1s than the lowest trait used, a successful roll gains a complication, and a failure turns into a botch.&quot;<br /> <br /> This also gets rid of the general impression that the cWOD mechanics &quot;punish&quot; high dice-pools.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-09T12:51:23-07:00","id":621567,"title":"","content":"In Shadowrun you have a &quot;botch&quot; every time you roll 1s on half the dice pool, but it doesn't negate a success, precisely as you're describing.<br /> <br /> Well, Shadowrun uses d6s, so I it should be worked a little, but the idea is still better than the current cWoD &quot;Rule of 1&quot;. I think your idea of botch when 1s are equal or more than the lowest trait used is a good one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-09T15:50:32-07:00","id":621666,"title":"","content":"I've been toying with it in various incarnations for a bit now. One of the reasons I like greater or equal to lowest trait, compared to half or dice-pool from SR, is it's more likely to actually happen with d10s, and provides a bit of a mechanical distinction between high/low and even trait characters.<br /> <br /> With the half dice-pool method, Someone with Dexterity 5/Medicine 1, Dex 1/Med 5, and Dex 3/Med 3 have the same issues with risking complications (and the same odds with the cWOD Rule of 1 methods).<br /> <br /> Using the lowest trait means even is preferable. A character that's got a surgeon's hands, but barely passed some first-aid training is very likely to mess something up, even if they do more good than harm. A character that can't hold their hand's steady, but it an expert doctor can easily slip a bit during an emergency surgery. Meanwhile someone that's above average in both is much more solid if not causing complications.<br /> <br /> It also avoids the need for making exceptions to botches for things like WP, Soak, and Damage rolls (if you even keep those last two rolls). Any single Trait roll needs to come up all 1s, which is rare enough to be worth a spectacular botch.<br /> <br /> Lastly, I've found it pretty nice for adding another widget for blessings/drawbacks. Weapons that are prone to being dangerous to their users can add 1s to the results (so that chainsaw might do a lot of damage, but it's really easy to bust the chain or worse). Instead of, &quot;your character can never botch rolls with X Ability,&quot; you can say, &quot;Ignore the first X number of 1s rolled for Ability X.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-09T17:54:15-07:00","id":621760,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition#post619900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3. I hope the Morality from nWoD make it into VtM and possibly the cWoD as a whole.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Humanity score that mortals have in VtM essentially <i>is</i> nWoD's Morality. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition#post619900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">4. I like alternating difficulties, but, the system should have caps. Any difficulty raise above 9 (due to circumstances) should reduce the dice pool, every difficulty lowered below 5 should give bonus dice. Difficulties below 5 or above 10 simply break the probabilities too much.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are two related issues here: botch mechanics, and overdriving the difficulty. Here's my thoughts:<br /> <div style=\"margin-left:40px\">I'd balance the botch mechanics by introducing the concept of the \u201cboost\u201d \u2014 the notion that 10s are special in a manner that mirrors the way 1s are special. Think of boosts as being a bit like the Enhancements being developed for the Sardonyx System: each boost lets you do a little something extra. Boosts and botches would cancel each other out: if you roll two botches and a boost, that's the same as rolling one botch; conversely, rolling three boosts and a botch is the same as rolling two boosts. Boosts and botches would be independent of success and failure: that is, botches don't cancel successes; and even when a botch from a 1 cancels the boost normally granted by a 10, it doesn't cancel the <i>success</i> granted by the 10. Conversely, successes don't negate botches: you can succeed and &quot;botch&quot; at the same sime, leading to a \u201cmessy success\u201d where you achieve your goal but something still goes wrong. <br /> <br /> Difficulty has a maximum and a minimum; the numbers are open for debate, though I\u2019d strongly recommend not setting the maximum higher than 9 or the minimum lower than 3; there should always be a chance to roll a regular success or failure on any given die. Driving the difficulty above the maximum starts negating successes instead of raising the target number (e.g., if you\u2019re three over the maximum, ignore the first three successes rolled), and driving the difficulty below the minimum starts granting bonus dice instead of lowering the target number (e.g., if you're three under the minimum, you get three additional dice in your pool).</div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-10T05:47:05-07:00","id":622081,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=621760#post621760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are two related issues here: botch mechanics, and overdriving the difficulty. Here's my thoughts:<br /> <div style=\"margin-left:40px\">I'd balance the botch mechanics by introducing the concept of the \u201cboost\u201d \u2014 the notion that 10s are special in a manner that mirrors the way 1s are special. Think of boosts as being a bit like the Enhancements being developed for the Sardonyx System: each boost lets you do a little something extra. Boosts and botches would cancel each other out: if you roll two botches and a boost, that's the same as rolling one botch; conversely, rolling three boosts and a botch is the same as rolling two boosts. Boosts and botches would be independent of success and failure: that is, botches don't cancel successes; and even when a botch from a 1 cancels the boost normally granted by a 10, it doesn't cancel the <i>success</i> granted by the 10. Conversely, successes don't negate botches: you can succeed and &quot;botch&quot; at the same sime, leading to a \u201cmessy success\u201d where you achieve your goal but something still goes wrong.<br /> <br /> Difficulty has a maximum and a minimum; the numbers are open for debate, though I\u2019d strongly recommend not setting the maximum higher than 9 or the minimum lower than 3; there should always be a chance to roll a regular success or failure on any given die. Driving the difficulty above the maximum starts negating successes instead of raising the target number (e.g., if you\u2019re three over the maximum, ignore the first three successes rolled), and driving the difficulty below the minimum starts granting bonus dice instead of lowering the target number (e.g., if you're three under the minimum, you get three additional dice in your pool).</div></div> </div> </div> </div>With a system sensible to the number of successes, I think the Boost rule is kinda redundant. Unless they make the system works with the idea of one success being enough, I would keep to <b>Heavy Arms</b>' idea. Having enough successes already counts for a boosted effect.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-10T12:37:41-07:00","id":622550,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=622081#post622081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With a system sensible to the number of successes, I think the Boost rule is kinda redundant. Unless they make the system works with the idea of one success being enough, I would keep to <b>Heavy Arms</b>' idea. Having enough successes already counts for a boosted effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>I never defined the specifics of what a Boost would do for you, other than the fact that each boost cancels out one botch (and vice versa). That is, in fact, my primary motivation for introducing boosts: to provide an equal and opposite counterweight to botches. 1s and 10s cancel, and 10s are good in a way similar to how 1s are bad; so increasing the size of the dice pool doesn\u2019t mean that more bad stuff will happen to you, the way that it does when there's nothing to cancel out your botches (e.g., 1e and 2e dice mechanics). Conversely, it\u2019s not as harsh as the Revised-era notion that a single success trumps all botches rolled, which resulted in botches very quickly becoming vanishingly rare as the pool size increased (which largely negates the point of having botch mechanics in the first place). <br /> <br /> The mechanical benefits of a boost <i>could</i> be as boring as \u201ceach boost increases the effective number of successes you generate by one\u201d, which would be similar to the \u201c10s count double toward success\u201d mechanic that I believe some of the non-WoD Storyteller games have used save for the fact that these extra successes would be vulnerable to being canceled out by botches. But that\u2019s not the only way to do it; you could also say that boosts enhance the results in ways that additional successes don't, either by specifying what multiple successes do for you and using boosts for anything else or by saying that boosts grant non-mechanical benefits much like botches cause non-mechanical complications. <br /> <hr />If boosts are a no-go, another possibility would be to say that 10s don't provide any sort of boost, per se, but that a single 10 (<i>not</i> a single success) negates all 1s. I'd have to run the numbers to know for sure; but my instincts say that this would drive the probability of a botch down as the dice pool grows much like the Revised rules do, but not as <i>quickly</i> as the Revised rules do. Heck, you could even keep the 1e/2e notion that uncanceled 1s subtract from successes. This could provide a middle ground between early editions' out-of-control botches and later editions' stifled botches.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-10T13:43:50-07:00","id":622613,"title":"","content":"Trying to come up with something for 10s to do (keeping in mind 10s either re-rolling or counting as two successes is already something special they do with Specialties), to balance out what 1s do, which is already somewhat problematic seems backwards. It's holding onto a mechanical sacred cow (1s have to have a negative impact on the roll), and then making the rules more complex to try to account for the issues it causes.<br /> <br /> Even if you don't like my idea, there's no reason a new edition of the cWOD needs to maintain the Rule of 1 sacred cow. It's free to develop a cleaner mechanic for generating complications and botches using other options.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-10T17:28:11-07:00","id":622876,"title":"","content":"Even if rules about 10s are covered by Specialties, they can implement them AND rework specialties.<br /> <br /> Lets be clear here, those rules shouldn't be exclusive to specialties, and more fit benefits for them could be applied.<br /> <br /> Maybe a specialty can ignore the Rule of 1 entirely, doesn't matter what form this take.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T07:03:09-07:00","id":623274,"title":"","content":"I've been thinking this a lot since the annoncement of V4, but what is the <b>purpose</b> of the rules ?<br /> Why having rules ? Is having rules can/should enforce the theme/mood of game, or not ?<br /> <br /> I'll try to explain : for me, the Rule of 1 is the most important rule of the cWod, because is emphasize the theme &quot;There will always be complications/you'll never truly win&quot;. This theme is the core of the &quot;gothic-punkness&quot; of the cWod. Remove this and what will distinguish the cWod from DnD, thematically speaking ? The dramatic failure add flavor to the game. <br /> <br /> Combat is another exemple : I've always think that combat rules where needlessly complicated to enforce the point that &quot;violence has a price&quot;. That's the great irony of W:tA : you play the greatest warriors, but violence will not be the solution against the Wyrm. Or this is supported by the fact that the Sabbat wins battle, but will ultimatly lose the war. Make combat a lot more simple and the whole &quot;violence has a price&quot; theme will be undermined (even if violence is necessary sometime, ence the drama). <br /> <br /> As a counterpoint, look at DnD. The theme of the game is &quot;Bang the door, Kill the monster, Loot the treasure&quot; (thanks Munchkins!) Yes, a lot of us have roleplay heavy games, but most of the rules are geared toward combat and are adept at covering combat situations. The levels (the character growth) is developped by combat almost exclusively. A &quot;1&quot; isn't a botch, but just &quot;you fail, no matter the bonus&quot;. <br /> <br /> It is not that rules can't be smoothered, improved (as V1 to V2 to VRev to V20), but the purpose of the rules is what should guide the changes. <br /> My only problem is I can't find something to improve. Except maybe don't give the Point-Blank bonus to Full-Auto shoot action. So many deads","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2140-asimar","name":"Asimar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2140_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T07:29:11-07:00","id":623283,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asimar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623274#post623274\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've been thinking this a lot since the annoncement of V4, but what is the <b>purpose</b> of the rules ?<br /> Why having rules ? Is having rules can/should enforce the theme/mood of game, or not ?<br /> <br /> I'll try to explain : for me, the Rule of 1 is the most important rule of the cWod, because is emphasize the theme &quot;There will always be complications/you'll never truly win&quot;. This theme is the core of the &quot;gothic-punkness&quot; of the cWod. Remove this and what will distinguish the cWod from DnD, thematically speaking ? The dramatic failure add flavor to the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>No. I don't think that this terrible failure of math is anything but another mechanic that could be replaced by one much better thought out than 1s. nWoD manages to have dramatic failure without it, so clearly, there are other ways. Also, D&amp;D is distinguished from cWoD by not being the same game, and that the WoD is generally for playing monsters rather than humans of any stripe of &quot;heroism&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Combat is another exemple : I've always think that combat rules where needlessly complicated to enforce the point that &quot;violence has a price&quot;. That's the great irony of W:tA : you play the greatest warriors, but violence will not be the solution against the Wyrm. Or this is supported by the fact that the Sabbat wins battle, but will ultimatly lose the war. Make combat a lot more simple and the whole &quot;violence has a price&quot; theme will be undermined (even if violence is necessary sometime, ence the drama). </div> </div> </div>Again, no, that's not required at all. The price of violence is in the consequences, not the system. Exalted 3rd edition has a much improved combat system meant for cinematic wire fighting and attacks that are way more powerful than they should be, but still features a critique of violence in the process by using the complications that follow a character's decisions. This theme is also present in Werewolf; read the essay that Ethan Skemp wrote for the 2nd edition player's guide. The Garou don't get punished by cosmic slaps on the wrist and presumably, game mechanics are also not there to fulfill this; only the aftermath of events serves as punishment. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As a counterpoint, look at DnD. The theme of the game is &quot;Bang the door, Kill the monster, Loot the treasure&quot; (thanks Munchkins!) Yes, a lot of us have roleplay heavy games, but most of the rules are geared toward combat and are adept at covering combat situations. The levels (the character growth) is developped by combat almost exclusively. A &quot;1&quot; isn't a botch, but just &quot;you fail, no matter the bonus&quot;.<br /> <br /> It is not that rules can't be smoothered, improved (as V1 to V2 to VRev to V20), but the purpose of the rules is what should guide the changes.<br /> My only problem is I can't find something to improve. Except maybe don't give the Point-Blank bonus to Full-Auto shoot action. So many deads </div> </div> </div>The rhetoric of &quot;go play D&amp;D!&quot;, no matter how it's presented, is extremely tiresome. cWoD games are already fundamentally not D&amp;D for taking place in a modern setting and putting you in control of monsters. It's thematically as far from D&amp;D as possible; if the rules make combat easier, the setting still wouldn't, since one of the consequences of violence is investigation of criminal acts by the authorities. You keep using violence and overt, unconcealed actions, and your own allies might just make you disappear.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T08:31:58-07:00","id":623345,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=620221#post620221\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm just tired of the idea that to keep the &quot;feel&quot; of the cWoD we have to stick with crappy rules, or translate the games to nWoD. I don't want the same rules as nWoD, I just want good rules for a change. And nWoD DOES have good examples that can be used as inspiration, not just copied.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not just the nWoD. It's predecessor, rules-wise, is Exalted (kind of two thirds way between Storyteller and Storytelling system), and I feel it has, too, a much better game flow, especially in combat, than the cWoD has.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T08:38:19-07:00","id":623349,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=621567#post621567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In Shadowrun you have a &quot;botch&quot; every time you roll 1s on half the dice pool, but it doesn't negate a success, precisely as you're describing.<br /> <br /> Well, Shadowrun uses d6s, so I it should be worked a little, but the idea is still better than the current cWoD &quot;Rule of 1&quot;. I think your idea of botch when 1s are equal or more than the lowest trait used is a good one.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, but playing SR4 it always seems like the ST has to come up with a kind of twist to make a success a not so successful success and often I feel it is brushed over. For, making up stuff that has next to little meaning in the game, and is kind of fair, also reduces the mood of the scene, in many cases... (Well, maybe my ST just isn't glibly or quick-witted enough...)<br /> <br /> I feel a cWoD would work well, if the difficulty was standardized to 7, with not more that two steps in either way, if a power reduces or adds difficulty. Then one could skip the 1 subtracts-rule, as there are already fewer successes but still have the revised &quot;no success and at least one 1 is a botch&quot; rule.<br /> <br /> I feel this would work very well, and be faster to calculate after a roll...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1008,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","user":"heinrich","date":". 08-11-2015, 11:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T08:47:21-07:00","id":623352,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=622081#post622081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With a system sensible to the number of successes, I think the Boost rule is kinda redundant. Unless they make the system works with the idea of one success being enough, I would keep to <b>Heavy Arms</b>' idea. Having enough successes already counts for a boosted effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree.<br /> Ending a roll with only one or two successes is a messy success essentially, since for many action, three successes are considered a good success.<br /> <br /> However, I don't like that rule much either, since for many powers to activate, one success is enough, and even for an attack one hit is enough to inflict the standard damage (not like only half for one success, 75% for two and normal damage by three successes, as this is a good success).<br /> <br /> So, the whole &quot;three successes scored is a good success&quot; rule is often broken within the rules of specific actions within the game and seems to mostly only apply to mundane attribute+ability actions.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T09:23:07-07:00","id":623375,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asimar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623274#post623274\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've been thinking this a lot since the annoncement of V4, but what is the <b>purpose</b> of the rules ?<br /> Why having rules ? Is having rules can/should enforce the theme/mood of game, or not ?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the <b>purpose </b>of the 1s subtracting successes is to even out the number of successes. Even a person with peak traits (dicepool 10) should not on average achieve phenomenal success on something he does. But with difficulty 6 he would achieve five successes on average, making the roll a phenomenal success. With the average one die showing the 1, this is an average of only four successes and that is seems to be in concert with mood, I think. The cWoD is in my mind an example of believable modern Fantasy.<br /> <br /> That said, I find the system unfortunate. It needs longer to evaluate a roll, than it does with only counting successes and evaluating the rolled 1s is kind of a bummer, every time.<br /> <br /> So, a system that has the difficulty in a range from 5 to 9 and with an standard of 7 would also reduce successes on an roll, making &quot;super success&quot; on a task a rarer occasion, while making the system faster and more rewarding.<br /> <br /> That said, I think that only mid-to-high level powers and exceptional circumstances should alter the difficulties, or that such changes should be limited to one step. Powers that allow for a difficulty to be changed by successes rolled steps simply break the system.<br /> The cWoD had dice pool adjustments for Wound penalties since ever, to have dice pool penalties or bonuses as results of powers used against or in favour of a character or as circumstance modifiers should be more present in the cWoD, imo.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T09:38:31-07:00","id":623379,"title":"","content":"Oh, and as I was writing in the Werewolf topic about IV Edition, I realised something else: Get rid of Appearance and Perception. The nWoD in this regard works way better.<br /> <br /> That said, <b>rip-off the social combat system</b> from 'A Song of Ice and Fire' RPG. Every god damn WoD game has a huge social part to it's setting, yet every game neglects to provide a system to use it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T12:20:47-07:00","id":623583,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623379#post623379\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh, and as I was writing in the Werewolf topic about IV Edition, I realised something else: Get rid of Appearance and Perception. The nWoD in this regard works way better.</div> </div> </div> </div>As a reminder, any way that the nWoD works is highly unlikely to be reproduced in 4th edition cWoD. Not only is Rose there to guide people away from that, if you want it, you already have the translation guides.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That said, <b>rip-off the social combat system</b> from 'A Song of Ice and Fire' RPG. Every god damn WoD game has a huge social part to it's setting, yet every game neglects to provide a system to use it. </div> </div> </div>You're going to need several different social systems, and they might not all line up so well between corporate boardrooms, high society galas, drug deals, and hostage negotiation. For that matter, Attributes probably won't line up well on an axis other than Physical, Social, and Mental; there's no guarantee of Power, Finesse, and Resistance neatly divided up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T12:47:56-07:00","id":623590,"title":"","content":"I'm tired of comparisons with DnD. They're usually completely out of context, proportion and purpose, full of prejudice and wrong assumptions. Seriously, if you think WoD define itself by being &quot;not-DnD&quot;, you didn't understood either.<br /> <br /> Giving more stability to difficulty is a good idea, but them you HAVE to rethink the Rule of 1. I don't like it, but can work with a difficulty of 6. 7 or higher will <u>need</u> to get rid of it, because this will make the rolls too unreliable. Of course it is relevant for game to make botch a possibility, but we all know the problems with the unreliability of high dice pools. 7 is good if we keep the gradation of successes. 8 is good if we assume 1 success as enough. One option for dif 8 is to keep the Rule of 1, but also implement the Boost idea for 10s instead of giving exceptional successes to high rolls.<br /> <br /> With powers being the only way to alter difficulty, 8 is also a good starting point because lessening difficulty will be way more common than raising it.<br /> <br /> Social systems are also a perfectly implementable thing. As you don't need whole new systems for each form of dealing physical harm, you don't need a separate system for each social context, just one system flexible enough. nWoD already have an excellent one in 2nd edition called Social Maneuvering. Again, there are no need to copy it, but it stands as proof that such is possible.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T13:06:16-07:00","id":623608,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623283#post623283\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The rhetoric of &quot;go play D&amp;D!&quot;, no matter how it's presented, is extremely tiresome. cWoD games are already fundamentally not D&amp;D for taking place in a modern setting and putting you in control of monsters. It's thematically as far from D&amp;D as possible; if the rules make combat easier, the setting still wouldn't, since one of the consequences of violence is investigation of criminal acts by the authorities. You keep using violence and overt, unconcealed actions, and your own allies might just make you disappear.</div> </div> </div> </div>You've misunderstood. It is not &quot;go play DnD&quot;, it was just for comparing two differents purpose of the rules. The Rule of 1 can be interpreted as theme enforcer or as a mathematical aberation, but, as a rule, it has to<i> mean</i> something, beyond the math behind. <br /> <br /> I've toyed with a lot of houserules for social combat for the various cWod in the years. One was about a &quot;rock-paper-scissor&quot; for Charisma-Manipulation-Appeareance. I've try rules where social atack where defended by the Virtues (Empathy vs Conscience/Wisdom/Entropy, Subterfuge vs Self-Control/Honor/Static, Intimadation vs Courage/Glory/Dynamic [I Have scalled the Renown to 5 instead of 10]), but each time, I ended up ditched them at the end. But it was just create social combat rules to give a mirror to the combat rules. I prefered the openess of the current rules, where Etiquette is important, not stat wise, but because it is a good sign in good society. <br /> <br /> All of that to said that the rule changes, I frankly don't know how they can do this. I don't even know what to change, even if I feel some changes are necessary. But I know that the rules will need a purpose, and this purpose should be true to the setting, theme and mood of the game, because even if the other editions rules where not optimum, it is the setting that pull me to the cWod.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2140-asimar","name":"Asimar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2140_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T13:30:40-07:00","id":623621,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623583#post623583\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a reminder, any way that the nWoD works is highly unlikely to be reproduced in 4th edition cWoD. Not only is Rose there to guide people away from that, if you want it, you already have the translation guides.</div> </div> </div> </div>The translation guides would really not be enough to play VtM with nWoD rules, or any cWoD game. They offer guidelines of who that could work, but if you actually wanted to do it, there would be a real load of work ahead of you.<br /> <br /> But, that is not necessarily what I would like to have. There isn't a need to switch completely to the God-Machine-Chronicles rules set for any new cWoD Edition. But, if there were to be more radical changes to the system mechanics, that are basically 25 years old, then switching the attributes should not be off the table. Obviously V20 was &quot;just&quot; a compilation, still Dodge and Linguistics were send home crying.<br /> <br /> One Power, One Finesse and One Resistance attribute for Physical, Social and Mental just makes sense and possibly even a forth group for spiritual/magic stuff. Just depending on how radical the overhaul should be. But I believe, we kind of deserve the cWoD Setting with an engine tune-up, badly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623583#post623583\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're going to need several different social systems, and they might not all line up so well between corporate boardrooms, high society galas, drug deals, and hostage negotiation. For that matter, Attributes probably won't line up well on an axis other than Physical, Social, and Mental; there's no guarantee of Power, Finesse, and Resistance neatly divided up.</div> </div> </div> </div>The 'A Song of Ice and Fire' system is for all social interaction of a given magnitude. Smaller stuff can be handled with a single roll, though. But that's not different to combat or physical challenges, that STs might handwave if of minor significance.<br /> <br /> But the system actually involves social &quot;hit points&quot; called &quot;Composure&quot;. And you have a kind of &quot;Social Armour&quot; called &quot;Intrigue Defense&quot;.<br /> So, if you want to Bargain, Charm, Convince, Incite, Intimidate, Seduce or Taunt a person the ST first determines what kind of relationship you and that person have at the beginning of the social battle, called &quot;Disposition&quot;, while the whole affair is called an &quot;Intrigue&quot;.<br /> The Disposition determines your Social Damage Reduction, as well bonuses or penalties one might get (usually, if you like someone, he has it easier to affect you (lower Damage Reduction, and you might be more willing to persuade the person, than lying to her, while a person you hate can say just about anything she wants without affecting you...)<br /> <br /> So a Player chooses one of the actions above, selecting if he is using persuasive or deceptive and rolls the appropriate attribute+skill combination. If bypassing the Intrigue Defense, damage (called &quot;Influence&quot;) is dealt. Influence is tied to another attribute, so Bargaining does Cunning attribute rating Influence damage, while Seduction would deal Influence damage tied to the Persuasion attribute. This damage is reduced by the Social Damage Reduction based on the Disposition and any remaining Influence is removed from the Composure. If the latter reaches zero, you have basically reached your goal with that person.<br /> <br /> Hope that gives a glimpse on the system, without knowing the mechanics of AFOIAF-RPG. Point is, that you can easily turn all kinds of social interactions into an Intrigue based combat scenario and you have relatively clear methods to evaluate the outcome of certain actions. And &quot;turns&quot; in this system can reach from the blink of an eye (in a scenario where taunting is relevant) to an hour at a banquet table, when trying to arrange a marriage. The system can be used for all, an possibly even be used alongside combat, if trying to intimidate an opponent, so he would flee.<br /> <br /> Obviously with the attributes, skills and other mechanics of the WoD such a system would need a complete rebuild, but it could work. And, frankly, I think a basic system is way less confusing, than having Werewolf's Staredown, Animal Attraction, Credibility, Fast-Talk, Interrogation, Intimidation, Oration, Performance, and Seduction all having their own rules entry in the Core Rules &quot;System and Drama&quot; section - with most not providing a system but leaving it to the ST to decide or make up a system on the fly.<br /> <br /> EDIT: I mean if each pack member would be tested in a challenge, an Ahroun would need a huge amount of game time for a combat challenge, since there are complicated rules. The Galliard rolles Manipulation + Performance once to see if he can make the Elder cry of joy or agony with his story. Hardly seems fair - drama wise...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1008,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","user":"heinrich","date":". 08-11-2015, 04:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T17:06:27-07:00","id":623761,"title":"","content":"I vote for simplification and consistency as the core focus of the crunchy bits.<br /> <br /> Introduce a system (or optional system) that uses the same information but far, far less dice. It would be excellent to have a system with two modes... a narrative mode, and a more technical mode. The rules should be slightly different for each, because the way they get played is very different in the real world.<br /> <br /> MAKE THE WORLD COHESIVE AND CONDUCIVE TO CROSSOVERS.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> That's my opinion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/PbrO9Kq.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2211-sixfootchicken","name":"SixFootChicken","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2211_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"559"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T20:28:55-07:00","id":623899,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=622613#post622613\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Trying to come up with something for 10s to do (keeping in mind 10s either re-rolling or counting as two successes is already something special they do with Specialties), to balance out what 1s do, which is already somewhat problematic seems backwards. It's holding onto a mechanical sacred cow (1s have to have a negative impact on the roll), and then making the rules more complex to try to account for the issues it causes.<br /> <br /> Even if you don't like my idea, there's no reason a new edition of the cWOD needs to maintain the Rule of 1 sacred cow. It's free to develop a cleaner mechanic for generating complications and botches using other options.</div> </div> </div> </div>Others have suggested reasons why botches ought to be kept; it's not a case of the rule of 1 being a sacred cow. What\u2019s needed is to find a way to keep it without having it either run wild or not come up often enough. And as the last part of my post indicates, there's a way to do that without the concept of boosts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T21:03:16-07:00","id":623906,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623899#post623899\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Others have suggested reasons why botches ought to be kept; it's not a case of the rule of 1 being a sacred cow.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nobody's stated a solid reason why the <i>mechanic</i> as it exists in the cWOD games needs to be kept. People have stated the need to maintain the resulting impact on the feel of the game the mechanic imparts.<br /> <br /> I've never argued against the idea of keeping botches as something that are part of the game. I don't think the cWOD 4e mechanics should go the nWOD way of basically, &quot;extremely rarely on their own, but players can opt into them.&quot; Botches should occur through the dice more frequently for a cWOD game.<br /> <br /> I'm arguing that (1) the Rule of 1 is a bad mechanic for this, and (2) adding more rules on top of the Rule of 1 to make up for its deficiencies is not good game design.<br /> <br /> Please, explain how, &quot;Instead of dropping rule X, we're going to add rule Y to balance out rule X,&quot; is not adding complexity to maintain a sacred cow. The only reason to consider your ideas for a &quot;Rule of 10&quot; is if you're already removing the concept of taking away the current Rule of 1 mechanical concept; hence it being a sacred cow.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What\u2019s needed is to find a way to keep it without having it either run wild or not come up often enough. </div> </div> </div>So... what's need is to consider it a sacred cow and make a bunch of mechanical changes to make it work, rather than consider any options that might accomplish the same mechanical impact without all this?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And as the last part of my post indicates, there's a way to do that without the concept of boosts. </div> </div> </div>It's irrelevant. Both options are still adding more rules to try to make a bad rule less bad, rather than look for a better rule to start with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T23:32:51-07:00","id":623948,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=622613#post622613\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Trying to come up with something for 10s to do (keeping in mind 10s either re-rolling or counting as two successes is already something special they do with Specialties), to balance out what 1s do, which is already somewhat problematic seems backwards. It's holding onto <u>a mechanical sacred cow (1s have to have a negative impact on the roll)</u>, and then making the rules more complex to try to account for the issues it causes.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've underlined what I understand to be your definition of the \u201csacred cow\u201d that you believe needs to be dropped, so that there's less chance of confusion. I'll note that it\u2019s more general than the Rule of 1, which is that <u>1s cancel successes, and that if you have more 1s than successes you botch (modified in the Revised editions so that a single 10 eliminates the possibility of a botch)</u> (underlined for ease of reference). <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623906#post623906\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nobody's stated a solid reason why the <i>mechanic</i> as it exists in the cWOD games needs to be kept. People have stated the need to maintain the resulting impact on the feel of the game the mechanic imparts.<br /> <br /> I've never argued against the idea of keeping botches as something that are part of the game. I don't think the cWOD 4e mechanics should go the nWOD way of basically, &amp;quot;extremely rarely on their own, but players can opt into them.&amp;quot; Botches should occur through the dice more frequently for a cWOD game.<br /> <br /> I'm arguing that (1) the Rule of 1 is a bad mechanic for this, and (2) adding more rules on top of the Rule of 1 to make up for its deficiencies is not good game design.</div> </div> </div> </div>For the record, I've never supported the notion of porting nWoD's \u201cvoluntary botches\u201d into the classic World of Darkness. If someone else has, please take it up with them. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623906#post623906\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Please, explain how, &amp;quot;Instead of dropping rule X, we're going to add rule Y to balance out rule X,&amp;quot; is not adding complexity to maintain a sacred cow. The only reason to consider your ideas for a &amp;quot;Rule of 10&amp;quot; is if you're already removing the concept of taking away the current Rule of 1 mechanical concept; hence it being a sacred cow.</div> </div> </div> </div>We seem to have had a miscommunication here. If you read my initial post a bit more carefully, I effectively <i>did</i> drop the Rule of 1, in that I said that in a boosts-and-botches system, 1s shouldn't cancel successes, only boosts. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623906#post623906\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So... what's need is to consider it a sacred cow and make a bunch of mechanical changes to make it work, rather than consider any options that might accomplish the same mechanical impact without all this?</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry; at the time I wrote that post, I was mentally conflating the Rule of 1 with the more general notion of \u201c1s push you toward a botch\u201d, as my group has long since houseruled the Rule of 1 out of existence, replacing it with \u201cif you roll more 1s than successes, you botch\u201d (back in the 2e days) and then \u201cif you roll any ones and no successes, you botch&quot; (after the Revised editions started coming out). But the bit about 1s canceling successes? Long gone, to the point that I had forgotten that that's the RAW. Please reread my post with that substitution in mind and see if you still have a problem with it. <br /> <br /> With that in mind, please read my \u201cboostless\u201d suggestion as \u201cif you've rolled more 1s than successes and no 10s, you botch.\u201d Again, nothing about 1s and successes canceling out, and with the notion of \u201cyou can succeed and \u2018botch\u2019 at the same time\u201d.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T00:28:33-07:00","id":623984,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623948#post623948\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've underlined what I understand to be your definition of the \u201csacred cow\u201d that you believe needs to be dropped, so that there's less chance of confusion. I'll note that it\u2019s more general than the Rule of 1, which is that <u>1s cancel successes, and that if you have more 1s than successes you botch (modified in the Revised editions so that a single 10 eliminates the possibility of a botch)</u> (underlined for ease of reference).</div> </div> </div> </div>Correct. What you underline is what I feel is the sacred cow. The Rule of 1 (which has multiple definitions due to multiple editions) is simply the current way the cWOD implements the concept.<br /> <br /> The concept itself should not be clung to as important. Which is not to say whatever rule does get implemented shouldn't use 1s as negatives (my own suggestion does after all). It's saying it shouldn't start from the design point where 1s <i>must</i> be negatives. The design consideration should be, &quot;what is the effect we want the mechanic to have,&quot; not, &quot;how can we make it so 1s have this effect.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We seem to have had a miscommunication here. </div> </div> </div>Possibly, however as I see it, you're simply dodging the point. You're not actually talking about how you're not treating what I've identified as the sacred cow in question as one, nor does going back and rereading your previous posts in the light of this one change any of the issues raised. Your explicit reasoning as stated for your boosts suggestion is, &quot;I'd balance the botch mechanics...&quot; that is, you'd add more rules to address a problem rule.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you read my initial post a bit more carefully, I effectively <i>did</i> drop the Rule of 1, in that I said that in a boosts-and-botches system, 1s shouldn't cancel successes, only boosts. </div> </div> </div>As you noted, my objection is not solely to the Rule of 1.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Please reread my post with that substitution in mind and see if you still have a problem with it. </div> </div> </div>Yes. It's still adding complexity with minimal - if any - actual pay off; especially if you're using the non-WOD ST system Rule of 1 where 1s don't cancel successes.<br /> <br /> The boosts concept is problematic because there's no need for boosts in the system (though the cWOD has repeatedly implemented this poorly). By having multiple successes equate to margin of success, there's already a way in the system to get extra effects. Thus they go back to their initial stated purpose of primarily existing just to avoid botches running out of control. Which could be addressed by fixing botches instead of adding boosts.<br /> <br /> The boostless suggestion has the same issue. It's adding another rule on top of rules that don't work right to try to fix things; rather than just fixing the root problem.<br /> <br /> To an extent, the problem I have is that you seem to be thinking like someone making house rules to patch the games to their liking, not thinking like a developer that's getting a chance to do a thorough change to the base mechanics. The idea that you'd just add more rules is natural when you're house ruling because the idea is to plug holes you feel need fixing. When you're looking at development ideas for a new edition, you can actually get to revamping things from the foundations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 08-12-2015, 03:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T01:54:54-07:00","id":623996,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asimar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">...But I know that the rules will need a purpose, and this purpose should be true to the setting, theme and mood of the game, because even if the other editions rules where not optimum, it is the setting that pull me to the cWod.</div> </div> </div> </div>No one argued that, nor here nor at any other thread about the 4th editions, as far as I remember.<br /> <br /> I don't know if the Attributes should be based on nWoD. What I do know is that Appearance and Perception have to be written off ASAP.<br /> <br /> You can make combat a LOT more simple by deducing the defensive dice-pool from the attack. And I would get rid of Soak rolls. Seriously, the only role of Aggravated Damage with the system working on Soak is to make Stamina the single most useless Attribute ever.<br /> <br /> By the way, Aggravated could be far less common. I like the idea of it demanding some actual effort, not only the right &quot;combat mode&quot;. Of course it doesn't count for a splat's weakness.<br /> <br /> What about the Rule of 1 and the Gradative Successes being changed for a Wyld Die? You could just have a single dice rolled at the same time. As base rule, if it rolls 1 the entire roll is a Botch (without needing to be a failure, mind you), if it rolls 10, the entire roll is a Boost, between 2 and 3 it is a Marginal Success, and from 4 to 9 you have a Complete Success. Some powers and situations can change the Wyld Die results instead of the dice pool, changing the chances of each quality of success instead of the base chances of success. Just a crude idea.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T03:52:08-07:00","id":624055,"title":"","content":"I'd stop 1s cancelling successes and raise the difficulty to 7. If a failure scores one or more 1s, then it's a botch. It's the same effect as in Revised, but without the subtraction.<br /> <br /> In essence, the tweaks to the system from Trinity and Exalted would be marvellous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T06:41:51-07:00","id":624218,"title":"","content":"Fixing the difficulty changes a lot throught all the games. Many roll are difficulty &quot;Willpower&quot;, or &quot;Opponent Wits+Occult&quot;, for exemple. And if you fix the difficulty number, how to represent difficulty (in the sense something should be easy/hard to accomplish) of a roll: change the dice pool. We are now leering into the nWod territory. Since OPP seems to keep the cWod and the nWod mechanics different, I doubt that this will be the solution.<br /> <br /> Also, I don't understand the hate for the Rule of 1. Yes, it has ruined countless of roll, but it also give our group some of the most memorable roleplay sequence in a game. I understand the maths behind why it is not ideal, but in itself, I don't see why it should invalidate it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2140-asimar","name":"Asimar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2140_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T09:37:55-07:00","id":624443,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623984#post623984\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Correct. What you underline is what I feel is the sacred cow. The Rule of 1 (which has multiple definitions due to multiple editions) is simply the current way the cWOD implements the concept.<br /> <br /> The concept itself should not be clung to as important. Which is not to say whatever rule does get implemented shouldn't use 1s as negatives (my own suggestion does after all). It's saying it shouldn't start from the design point where 1s <i>must</i> be negatives. The design consideration should be, \u201cwhat is the effect we want the mechanic to have,\u201d not, \u201chow can we make it so 1s have this effect.\u201d</div> </div> </div> </div>So in other words, it doesn't matter to you what rule I come up with or how well it works; all that matters is whether my motivation was right? That is, because I mentioned \u201cbalancing the botch mechanics\u201d, it doesn\u2019t matter that what I\u2019ve come up with bears only a passing resemblance to the actual botch mechanics; I'm condemned for merely choosing to use the existing botch mechanics as a starting point. Heck, it doesn't even matter that the botch mechanics that I started with aren't even the botch mechanics as written, but are in fact a house ruled replacement for them that's been in use by my group for so long that I forgot that they <i>aren't</i> the RAW; the fact that I started with what I <i>thought</i> were the RAW renders whatever I came up with in the end moot, because I\u2019m \u201ctaking a sacred cow and adding to it\u201d. <br /> <br /> Heck, even the fact that one of the things I came up with is <i>very nearly identical to what <u>you</u> came up with</i> doesn\u2019t seem to matter: I did it the wrong way, so it doesn't count. <br /> <br /> That's what you seem to be saying. If so, I'm never going to convince you that I'm not holding onto a sacred cow, no matter what I say; so I won't try to defend against the charge any further; I'll just say \u201cwhatever\u201d whenever you bring it up. <br /> <br /> If you\u2019re willing to consider things in terms of results instead of motivations, please compare the following options:<br /> <br /> \u2022 1s cancel successes; you botch if you have more 1s than successes. <br /> \u2022 1s cancel successes; you botch if you get no successes and any 1s. <br /> \u2022 you botch if you get more 1s than successes and no 10s. You can botch and succeed simultaneously. <br /> \u2022 you botch if you get more 1s than the lowest trait in the pool. You can botch and succeed simultaneously. <br /> <br /> The first is the RAW in 2e; the second is the RAW in Revised; the third is my \u201cboostless\u201d option; and the fourth is a paraphrase of your option. What are the actual <i>results</i> of each of these rules? <br /> <br /> \u2022 the first two options tend to suppress the number of successes achieved, with the resulting statistics for success resembling what would happen if you didn't have 1s cancel successes and instead jacked up all difficulties by 1. <br /> \u2022 the third and fourth options have no effect on the number of successes achieved; that's determined solely by the difficulty rating and the size of the dice pool. They also both share the concept of being able to botch (i.e., \u201cthings get complicated\u201d) and succeed at the same time. <br /> \u2022 the first option results in the likelihood of a botch increasing as the size of the dice pool increases. <br /> \u2022 the second option results in the likelihood of a botch becoming vanishingly small unless the difficulty is very high and the dice pool is very small. <br /> \u2022 the third option results in the likelihood of a botch going down as the size of the dice pool increases, but at a more modest rate than the second option. <br /> \u2022 the fourth option results in the likelihood of a botch going up with the number of traits that comprise the dice pool*. The effect of the size of the pool on the likelihood of a botch also depends on the number of traits contributing to it, with the overall trend either holding steady at zero (for one trait) or increasing as the number of chances to get a 1 increase faster than the number of 1s you need to get in order to botch. <br /> <br /> * if you're rolling a seven-dice Humanity or Willpower pool, you won't get a botch even if you roll all 1s; but if you're rolling a seven-dice Intelligence + Occult + Path pool, three 1s will definitely suffice for a botch and two 1s might even be enough. <br /> <br /> Is there anything I missed in my analysis? <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623984#post623984\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To an extent, the problem I have is that you seem to be thinking like someone making house rules to patch the games to their liking, not thinking like a developer that's getting a chance to do a thorough change to the base mechanics. The idea that you'd just add more rules is natural when you're house ruling because the idea is to plug holes you feel need fixing. When you're looking at development ideas for a new edition, you can actually get to revamping things from the foundations.</div> </div> </div> </div>I\u2019ll admit to having a preference for a minimalist approach when it comes to messing with the rules, in that radical changes tend to lead to many unintended consequences which need to be tracked down and addressed in turn, potentially leading to even more unintended consequences.* This is just as true when developing a new edition as it is when creating a house rule; possibly moreso, because there\u2019s so much more of a temptation to get carried away when all of the rules are candidates for change. <br /> <br /> * For instance, I suspect you were assuming a two-trait pool when you devised your suggestion for an alternative to the botch mechanics; that made the immunity to botching of a one-trait pool an unintended consequence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T09:45:07-07:00","id":624446,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=623996#post623996\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know if the Attributes should be based on nWoD. What I do know is that Appearance and Perception have to be written off ASAP.</div> </div> </div> </div>Appearance can be fixed. It's actually pretty simple:<br /> <br /> 1) Ditch the first impression rules; almost everyone house rules them out anyway.<br /> <br /> 2) Redefined Appearance from &quot;How pretty are you?&quot; to &quot;How well do you use your physicality in social situations?&quot; This is something some of the non-WOD ST system games have started down the path of already.<br /> <br /> Perception is definitely the first Attribute I would put on the chopping block. Especially if it got as something like Resolve/Composure to not have everything go to Willpower for non-physical &quot;defense.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What about the Rule of 1 and the Gradative Successes being changed for a Wyld Die? </div> </div> </div>Seems too big a departure from refining the system to outright changing it. The &quot;Wyld Die&quot; becomes way too dominant on results. &quot;I got 10 successes!, but I rolled a 2 on my Wyld Die, so it's basically the same as if I rolled 1 success... yay?&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asimar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=624218#post624218\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, I don't understand the hate for the Rule of 1. Yes, it has ruined countless of roll, but it also give our group some of the most memorable roleplay sequence in a game. I understand the maths behind why it is not ideal, but in itself, I don't see why it should invalidate it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because the math behind it if far worse than &quot;not ideal.&quot;<br /> <br /> The hate on it is because it it complexity with minimal actual pay off. There are much more elegant ways to achieve the effect we want (those memorable role-play sequences). There are also countless exceptions in the books to avoid botches screwing with various rolls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T14:19:18-07:00","id":624642,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asimar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=624218#post624218\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fixing the difficulty changes a lot throught all the games. Many roll are difficulty &quot;Willpower&quot;, or &quot;Opponent Wits+Occult&quot;, for exemple. And if you fix the difficulty number, how to represent difficulty (in the sense something should be easy/hard to accomplish) of a roll: change the dice pool. We are now leering into the nWod territory. Since OPP seems to keep the cWod and the nWod mechanics different, I doubt that this will be the solution.</div> </div> </div> </div>In all cases of such difficulties determined by the opponents traits use: 4+(trait)/2.<br /> <br /> That is 4+Willpower/2 or 4+(Wits+Occult)/2 respectively.<br /> <br /> Such a mechanic would also keep every 2nd, revised or W20 source material easily convert-able.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=624446#post624446\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perception is definitely the first Attribute I would put on the chopping block. Especially if it got as something like Resolve/Composure to not have everything go to Willpower for non-physical &quot;defense.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually often go to Gnosis as a defence if I improvise and spritual or &quot;soul stamina&quot; seems appropriate. But, sure, for all social or mental challenges I would love to have Resolve/Composure around in cWoD, too.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T15:12:03-07:00","id":624657,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=624443#post624443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So in other words, it doesn't matter to you what rule I come up with or how well it works</div> </div> </div> </div>I said no such thing. I know when we disagree, you tend to start reading my posts with 100% extra non-present vitriol and all, but it is very trying to have our disagreements frequently disrupted by this sort of insulting nonsensical strawman.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm condemned for merely choosing to use the existing botch mechanics as a starting point. </div> </div> </div>First off, if you are going to claim personal insult because of criticisms of your design suggestions? Head off-line and chill out. Failure to separate criticisms of your posted idea, and criticisms of your personally is generally a sign it's time to step away from this for a bit.<br /> <br /> Second, there's the whole additional set of criticisms I gave that you're ignoring. You're treating my general design approach point as my sole response, and ignoring my direct criticisms of your ideas as if I didn't make them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Heck, even the fact that one of the things I came up with is <i>very nearly identical to what <u>you</u> came up with</i> doesn\u2019t seem to matter: I did it the wrong way, so it doesn't count. </div> </div> </div>It's not nearly identical, no matter how much you want to pan back and paraphrase to paint it that way. You're ignoring my actual design issue with your posted ideas because you're conflating it with my general designs issue with treating 1s as negatives as a sacred cow.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's what you seem to be saying. </div> </div> </div>Based on what? Besides a reading the clearly skips a section of my post?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> \u2022 the first option results in the likelihood of a botch increasing as the size of the dice pool increases.<br /> \u2022 the second option results in the likelihood of a botch becoming vanishingly small unless the difficulty is very high and the dice pool is very small. </div> </div> </div>Both of these are incorrect; or at least incomplete. The first option has botches statistically more frequently, but the overall effect with increasing dice-pools at the same difficulty remains the same: The odds of success go up, and the odds of botching generally go down (baring odd humps at higher difficulties in the botch curves), but the ratio of botches to failures consistently grows in favor of botches (aka if you fail, you'll botch more than not as dice-pools increase).<br /> <br /> The RAW options, essentially, have the same statistically problems. The earlier one simply makes it stronger.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> \u2022 the third option results in the likelihood of a botch going down as the size of the dice pool increases, but at a more modest rate than the second option. </div> </div> </div>It's somewhat impossible to flat out say this as fact. The main thing it does mechanically, is divorce the odds of botching from the difficulty of the roll. How this compares in terms of rate is difficult because you can't presume a given rate for the first two options since there's a different rate for each difficulty level.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> \u2022 the fourth option results in the likelihood of a botch going up with the number of traits that comprise the dice pool*. The effect of the size of the pool on the likelihood of a botch also depends on the number of traits contributing to it, with the overall trend either holding steady at zero (for one trait) or increasing as the number of chances to get a 1 increase faster than the number of 1s you need to get in order to botch. </div> </div> </div>The first point is true, but it's the cWOD... not the nWOD. You roll one trait, or two traits, with possible bonus dice. There aren't generally three trait rolls in the cWOD like in the nWOD (even if often shorthanded that way for powers that grant bonus dice equal to their rating; such as V20 Potence).<br /> <br /> And yes, the idea is that in a two Trait roll your odds of botches goes up if you focus on one trait and leave the other low. That's part of the motivation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is there anything I missed in my analysis? </div> </div> </div>You're paraphrasing my suggestion wrong. It's equal to or greater than, not greater than. So single dice pool rolls can botch (even if extremely rarely; though again, a large number of those rolls are already RAW exceptions in the systems).<br /> <br /> As well, you skipped one of my main criticisms of your proposal: it is adding more rules (I need to pay attention to 10s now for basic resolution).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I\u2019ll admit to having a preference for a minimalist approach when it comes to messing with the rules, in that radical changes tend to lead to many unintended consequences which need to be tracked down and addressed in turn, potentially leading to even more unintended consequences. </div> </div> </div>That's why it is so important to avoid sacred cows if you're doing a full revision of a game where you can restart the rules from the ground up instead of just patching things. Not just tossing in things because that's how the previous edition did it that way, means you avoid the unintended consequences cycle. You develop (which does mean having to work on new rules for a bit to make sure they work right) from the basics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For instance, I suspect you were assuming a two-trait pool when you devised your suggestion for an alternative to the botch mechanics; that made the immunity to botching of a one-trait pool an unintended consequence. </div> </div> </div>Your suspicion is incorrect. When I was working on the idea, it I was assuming cWOD standard rules that would mean dealing with both single and two trait rolls. <br /> <br /> The unintended &quot;consequence&quot; was the idea of increasing the number of 1s a roll gets by default as a tool for modelling dangerous things (and later modelling certain blessings), because I was looking for a way to keep (a modified) WtA Frenzy check, and Arete rolls maintain their risk.<br /> <br /> Paradox loses a lot of its bite when Arete has to roll all 1s to botch, so I was working on moving some of the difficulty modifiers into &quot;# of 1s&quot; modifiers instead (specifically vulgarity and domino effect). I actually like the results a lot. So unintended, it was a happy result none-the-less.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=624642#post624642\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I actually often go to Gnosis as a defence if I improvise and spritual or &quot;soul stamina&quot; seems appropriate.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can see that with Werewolf. Frankly, given Garou generally having fairly low starting WP ratings, and very few mental defense powers, I'd love to see something like that for WtA. Though it gets complicated to take that out to the rest of the WOD.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T17:24:16-07:00","id":624769,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=624657#post624657\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can see that with Werewolf. Frankly, given Garou generally having fairly low starting WP ratings, and very few mental defense powers, I'd love to see something like that for WtA. Though it gets complicated to take that out to the rest of the WOD.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think you are right on Garou having in general low WP ratings. They have starting values by tribe of 3 or 4 and it costs them one freebie to raise that starting score. With vampires Willpower starts with the Courage score and that often isn't higher than 3 or 4 either. That said, with Gnosis and Rage the garou have two traits to spend XP on, that vampires have not, while they have three virtues. All in all, their Willpower should on average just be as high as a vampire's. Or any other cWoD creature, besides a human's 2 to 4 average.<br /> <br /> And that is, where difficulties tied to Willpower are often not desirable. So, I like my idea of a standard difficulty of 4+(Conter-Dicepool)/2.<br /> So difficulties from 5 to 9, usually, that is where the system works best, imo.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T18:18:06-07:00","id":624858,"title":"","content":"Vampires (and games based on VtM Virtues) at creation have 1-5 Willpower before freebies; starting on a Path requires being at 5 after freebies.<br /> <br /> Garou have 3-4.<br /> <br /> Mages and Socerers are 5.<br /> <br /> Wraiths are 5 too IIRC (as a note, I'm doing this from memory and some notes, not straight out of the books).<br /> <br /> Changelings are 1, 2, or 5 (just doing the core ones).<br /> <br /> Demons are 4-5.<br /> <br /> Kindred of the East have to start with 5 regardless of the base they manage.<br /> <br /> Hunters are 3 (though 21 freebies).<br /> <br /> Mummies (from the Rev. book) are 5.<br /> <br /> Orpheus is kind of nuts with a range of 4 to 9 (though they get hit hard elsewhere).<br /> <br /> So, in general, the Garou are on the low end of the supernaturals in terms of starting WP. Not the lowest, but below average at least.<br /> <br /> ----------<br /> <br /> I'm not arguing away changing the use of WP as difficulty. Frankly, I'd rather get rid of difficulties based on the target's traits in general. If something can be contested, let it be rolled against instead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T02:28:57-07:00","id":625253,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asimar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=624218#post624218\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fixing the difficulty changes a lot throught all the games. Many roll are difficulty &quot;Willpower&quot;, or &quot;Opponent Wits+Occult&quot;, for exemple. And if you fix the difficulty number, how to represent difficulty (in the sense something should be easy/hard to accomplish) of a roll: change the dice pool. We are now leering into the nWod territory. Since OPP seems to keep the cWod and the nWod mechanics different, I doubt that this will be the solution.<br /> <br /> Also, I don't understand the hate for the Rule of 1. Yes, it has ruined countless of roll, but it also give our group some of the most memorable roleplay sequence in a game. I understand the maths behind why it is not ideal, but in itself, I don't see why it should invalidate it.</div> </div> </div> </div>You can turn difficulties based upon target's traits into resisted rolls.<br /> <br /> And what everyone is trying to discuss here is precisely a better rule to obtain the intended effect of the Rule of 1. It is a marvelous effect, but a bad rule. Why not try to find a better one?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Appearance can be fixed. It's actually pretty simple:<br /> <br /> 1) Ditch the first impression rules; almost everyone house rules them out anyway.<br /> <br /> 2) Redefined Appearance from &quot;How pretty are you?&quot; to &quot;How well do you use your physicality in social situations?&quot; This is something some of the non-WOD ST system games have started down the path of already.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is almost exactly Presence from nWoD, and already how I use it on my games.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T02:50:37-07:00","id":625265,"title":"","content":"Appearance should just go. A Social Stamina trait would make much more sense. Appearance could then be rolled into Charisma (where it belongs).<br /> <br /> Likewise, Perception could be rolled into Intelligence (deductive, investigative) and Wits (responsive, reflexive). Then you can have a Mental Stamina trait too.<br /> <br /> Willpower would become primarily used for its points, but that's fine by me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T07:06:14-07:00","id":625418,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=625253#post625253\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You can turn difficulties based upon target's traits into resisted rolls.</div> </div> </div> </div>Doesn't make the game faster. And it gets a lot more chance involved again.<br /> <br /> I, as an ST, don't need to or want to roll for NPCs at all. Just the way I like it. So I'd rather have the player make defensive rolls, depending on how badass I think the NPCs power is, and offensive rolls based on how difficult I think it should be to affect the NPC.<br /> <br /> So, personally, I would let the Player roll all the time. If he dominates another person, roll against 4+(willpower)/2. If he gets dominated roll Willpower against 4+(Manipulation+Leadership)/2 of the target. In any case, the NPCs don't roll. That gives Players the subjective impression. that the fate of their characters is in their hands.<br /> <br /> But, I don't think that OPP would go for such a system. It would usually work that the ST rolls for the NPCs and that any action (combat, supernatural, whatever) has a roll against a difficulty, with resisted rolls being limited to occasions where both parties actively try to best the other party.<br /> <br /> <br /> nWoD, iirc, has the power attribute in the social column named &quot;Presence&quot;, which includes physical appearance and Charisma - I wouldn't mind such a thing, with real physical beauty/ugliness being represented by modifiers and appear in the rules as merits/flaws.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T15:30:24-07:00","id":625991,"title":"","content":"Given that they will probably keep the standard &quot;NPCs follow the same rules as PCs&quot;, making any roll be one-sided means that sometimes the ST will roll all the dice. So, as a standard rule, I would make all those rolls resisted.<br /> <br /> That said, an optional rule to put all the rolls in the hands of the players would be a nice addition.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T15:37:14-07:00","id":626000,"title":"","content":"Definitely not, sometimes as ST you want a little ability to fudge the rolls, you can't if you don't make any.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3075-coridan","name":"Coridan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3075_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"528"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T15:37:57-07:00","id":626002,"title":"","content":"I'd rather have the virtue and vice system over the nature and demeanor one myself. Easier to handle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my DriveThruRPG books at: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse.php?author=Carrie%20Kube\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse....=Carrie%20Kube</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1834-yaoi-huntress-earth","name":"Yaoi Huntress Earth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1834_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"535"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T15:50:22-07:00","id":626011,"title":"","content":"Personally if they do a CWoD rulebook i wish for<br /> <br /> Re-do the combat system. Personally i preffer they go with the shadowrun route and instead of &quot;attack roll-&gt;damage roll-&gt;soaking roll&quot; go with &quot;attack roll-&gt;defense roll-&gt;soaking roll&quot;. Its more organic that way and doesnt give the situation in which an awesome attack roll get nullified by a crappy damage one.<br /> <br /> Use &quot;WoD combat&quot; is a book that is the owod equivalent of armory/armory reloaded. Bring the combat styles back for use. I hate that there seem to be only 4 combat styles in whole world with rules which are all supernatural.<br /> <br /> Get rid of perception as an stat and just go with a merit/background. Stop making merit/flaws optional.<br /> <br /> This one is so old and i seem it houseruled so many times i forgot its there. Stop with the priorities for chargen. Just give us point in attribute and skills to do assign as we please.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 08-13-2015, 06:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T12:23:24-07:00","id":626790,"title":"","content":"I was never bothered by manoeuvres. But I've always house ruled the priorities thing at character creation. It always struck me as overly restrictive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T12:47:10-07:00","id":626830,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=625265#post625265\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perception could be rolled into Intelligence (deductive, investigative) and Wits (responsive, reflexive).</div> </div> </div> </div> What accounts for the fact that there are people with keener eyesight, hearing, olfactory, tactile, and gustatory senses than other people? What amount of Wits or Intelligence is going to relevantly allow you to discern a particular shape at a great distance; or allow you to accurately determine the direction from where a relatively slight sound came; or isolate a particular smell in a room? I agree that Perception is a little overpowered--but improving one's Intelligence and Wits (i.e., stats that reflect primarily cognitive abilities) isn't going to help hone physical senses.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T13:55:41-07:00","id":626947,"title":"","content":"Perception is a complex mental process, with indeed little to do with actual senses. Some people with heavy impairments on sensory input have quite good reaction and attention for details, that is what the Attribute actually accounts for.<br /> <br /> Also, it is not overpowered, it is more like UNDERpowered as an Attribute. Not to say it is effectively silly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T14:34:00-07:00","id":626979,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=626830#post626830\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> What accounts for the fact that there are people with keener eyesight, hearing, olfactory, tactile, and gustatory senses than other people? What amount of Wits or Intelligence is going to relevantly allow you to discern a particular shape at a great distance; or allow you to accurately determine the direction from where a relatively slight sound came; or isolate a particular smell in a room? I agree that Perception is a little overpowered--but improving one's Intelligence and Wits (i.e., stats that reflect primarily cognitive abilities) isn't going to help hone physical senses.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem with this argument is that we have Abilities and Merits. We have Alertness to judge how good your basic senses are, as well as a number of other Abilities to more specific applications. We have the Acute Senses Merit for characters that are keener with some senses than others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T14:54:26-07:00","id":626991,"title":"","content":"Given that Stamina is already described as partly mental, perhaps we can have overlapping Attributes. In addition to their original categories, Stamina and Manipulation are also Mental*, Perception and Appearance are also Physical**, and Dexterity and Wits are also Social***.<br /> <br /> *: Stamina is already like nWoD Resolve and has explicitly been so since Revised, and Manipulation involves playing angles toward a desired outcome.<br /> **: Pretty self-explanatory, really; Appearance is related to a number of physical features and responses, while Perception involves sensitivity to the physical environment.<br /> ***: Dexterity is there as the flip-side of Appearance, while Wits, as grace under fire, has a place in staying ahead of others and grasping details quickly enough for conversation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T15:24:46-07:00","id":627047,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=625991#post625991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, an optional rule to put all the rolls in the hands of the players would be a nice addition.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. And if it was an option, it was even better.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Coridan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=626000#post626000\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Definitely not, sometimes as ST you want a little ability to fudge the rolls, you can't if you don't make any.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, but there wouldn't be a problem if I have the option to let the player roll, or roll as ST, depending on what I feel is appropriate.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yaoi Huntress Earth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=626002#post626002\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd rather have the virtue and vice system over the nature and demeanor one myself. Easier to handle.</div> </div> </div> </div>I also agree.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T15:29:47-07:00","id":627050,"title":"","content":"I would hope the would stop making gift rolls that involve non-living matter with Manipulation. Sure, one could argue the garou does manipulate the spirit of a baseball bat to become a tennis rack, but this explanation is even more far-fetched when there was no awoken spirit in the bat to begin with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T22:45:00-07:00","id":627281,"title":"","content":"The Attribute/Skill selection for powers could use tightening up all over the place really.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T02:00:19-07:00","id":627381,"title":"","content":"Perception can be covered by Merits and Abilities, as others have said.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T12:05:35-07:00","id":627748,"title":"","content":"Alertness and Awareness should be turned into one. They're both relevant enough for you to buy but, seriously, they are rather limited compared to other Abilities. I don't think we need the same skill set from nWoD, but the general idea of a set of more generic abilities is a good one. Specially when Secondary Abilities enter the mix.<br /> <br /> By the way, the Ability Categories are more than dated. Sometimes they don't make sense at all...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T13:44:29-07:00","id":627887,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=627748#post627748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By the way, the Ability Categories are more than dated. Sometimes they don't make sense at all...</div> </div> </div> </div>Or are different between games, since some games needed other abilities in a given category.<br /> <br /> A little cleanup on the abilities would be nice, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T13:51:30-07:00","id":627890,"title":"","content":"And, if possible, a little universality. At least for main Abilities.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T14:37:02-07:00","id":627930,"title":"","content":"Getting away from redundant and Secondary Abilities is always a plus for me.<br /> <br /> This is one of the reasons I tend to like the Dark Ages Revised core rule-set, even over the 20th ones.<br /> <br /> It added a very simple way to address these (and it's cWOD that predates any nWOD stuff, so despite any similarities... the cWOD did it first): Specialties and Expertise.<br /> <br /> DARev Ability Specialties are not linked to a dot rating, but purchased separately for +1 dice to the pool when it applies. An Expertise has the same mechanical bonus, but essentially take the place of Secondaries for Abilities that are very broad. So when you buy Crafts 1 (either at creation or with XP) you also pick an Expertise to guide what kind of crafting you actual do, and you'd have to buy more Expertise traits to cover broader ranges.<br /> <br /> It takes a bit of work to define these to make it clear what should be a Specialty and what should be an Expertise, but it isn't that hard. In the past I used a simple rule of thumb: Specialties are &quot;verbs&quot;and Expertise traits are &quot;nouns.&quot; Of course, semantically, you can always bend things one way or the other. But the idea is that a Specialties apply to actions, while an Expertise is a sub-division of an Ability based on scope.<br /> <br /> This method makes combining various traits easier.<br /> <br /> To keep on the Alertness thing:<br /> <br /> Make the base ability Intuition. You're relying on your gut and general experience, rather than any specific training. You can then have Expertise traits like Alertness (training in a more systematic analysis of sensory input), Awareness (knowing how to separate supernatural sensory inputs from mundane ones), Empathy (training in reading the complexities of human emotional expression), and Instinct/Primal Urge (Understanding sensory inputs coming from animal sources via shapeshifting or riding an animal, etc.).<br /> <br /> It's also pretty easy to tweak. For groups that don't really want to care, they can just ignore Expertise traits and slam it all into one Ability. Groups that feel some Abilities are broad enough that should have this sort of granularity, but not all of them need it (probably the default assumption) can use them for some Abilities but not others. And some groups might want them for every Ability.<br /> <br /> You can then drop having some for of Ability sorting algorithm. The main mechanical point of those categories is the penalty for being untrained. That can just be moved to the Expertise system. And then you just have a single list of Abilities without trying to have some sort of quasi-consistent idea of which falls into which.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T18:30:39-07:00","id":628165,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=627890#post627890\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, if possible, a little universality. At least for main Abilities.</div> </div> </div> </div>True. Would be cool if there were like 5 to 7 main abilities and 1 to 3 splat related abilities, like Rituals, Kenning, Primal-Urge, and stuff like that. For splats that don't need them, there would simply be more lines for secondary skills.<br /> <br /> While I like the idea of Expertises ans Specialities like described in the DA rules, there seems to still be a need for some abilities that simply aren't subdivisions of other traits. That is unless one specifically divides &quot;Expertises&quot; into such, that only grant the +1 dicepool bonus but would have been allowed to be rolled with the basic trait nonetheless, and some that need to be bought in order to &quot;activate&quot; that scope of the skill. Like man-used &quot;heavy weapons&quot; being part of Firearms and possibly may be rolled in any case with that trait, while using an artillery is subject to the Computer skill, but requires the Expertise to be bought.<br /> <br /> Well, it would be some tweaking, but it could work. But I don't mind the secondary skills that much.<br /> <br /> That said, the cWoD would really need guidelines on how fast characters are supposed to pick up new traits. The mechanics for that have always been hidden away with the Instruction secondary skill...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T08:49:12-07:00","id":631236,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=627930#post627930\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Getting away from redundant and Secondary Abilities is always a plus for me.<br /> <br /> [...]<br /> <br /> DARev Ability Specialties are not linked to a dot rating, but purchased separately for +1 dice to the pool when it applies. An Expertise has the same mechanical bonus, but essentially take the place of Secondaries for Abilities that are very broad. So when you buy Crafts 1 (either at creation or with XP) you also pick an Expertise to guide what kind of crafting you actual do, and you'd have to buy more Expertise traits to cover broader ranges.<br /> <br /> [...]<br /> <br /> Specialties are &quot;verbs&quot;and Expertise traits are &quot;nouns.&quot; Of course, semantically, you can always bend things one way or the other. But the idea is that a Specialties apply to actions, while an Expertise is a sub-division of an Ability based on scope.<br /> <br /> This method makes combining various traits easier.<br /> <br /> To keep on the Alertness thing:<br /> <br /> Make the base ability Intuition. You're relying on your gut and general experience, rather than any specific training. You can then have Expertise traits like Alertness (training in a more systematic analysis of sensory input), Awareness (knowing how to separate supernatural sensory inputs from mundane ones), Empathy (training in reading the complexities of human emotional expression), and Instinct/Primal Urge (Understanding sensory inputs coming from animal sources via shapeshifting or riding an animal, etc.).</div> </div> </div> </div>Perfect! Abilities bloat fixed in one go! The aura perception included in M20 Awareness would presumably disappear as a result, but that wouldn't bother me. I feel like that should be a magickal Effect anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T10:45:34-07:00","id":631304,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=627748#post627748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Alertness and Awareness should be turned into one. They're both relevant enough for you to buy but, seriously, they are rather limited compared to other Abilities.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have to disagree on that particular point. I've played WoD since Day One (and slightly before, technically, due to a couple different play-testings) back in '91, and I think Alertness is as useful as most (not all, but most) other Abilities. Similarly, the distinction between Alertness and Awareness is a critical one, as one has to do with the mundane/physical while the other has to do with the supernatural/metaphysical (and before we go down the road of how various powers do &quot;x, y and z&quot;, the Awareness trait represents a level of metaphysical intuition/astuteness that theoretically could be possessed by almost anyone *without* their having to have various powers that might not be as appropriate to their concept/implementation... not unlike how Arcane has a distinct flavor/purpose that is separate from Obfuscate, Blur of the Milky Eye/Blissful Ignorance, et al...). Now, as stated later on and in other threads, there is a validity to the argument that the two should be combined into Intuition, but then I think that conceptually overpowers the Ability: it would be similar to combining Medicine with Science (Biology/what-have-you) -- a doctor could be an excellent physician (Medicine:4) but only having functional biological knowledge outside of his applied practice vis-a-vis biological research/study (Biology:2); similarly, it would be like combining Computers with Technology/Repair -- there are plenty of highly competent (Computers:3+) programmers who only have functional knowledge of repairing/replacing the hardware involved (Tech:1 or 2), just as conversely there are plenty of engineers who may have Tech:4 but &quot;only&quot; have Computers:2 or (appropriate)Science: X = &lt;Tech). It's a similar concept here. Alertness and Awareness both have to do with (P)erception, but in two very different ways. (That distinction has come up several times in the DA/Ravenloft hybrid I've been running again this summer for some friends, for example.)<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=627748#post627748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By the way, the Ability Categories are more than dated. Sometimes they don't make sense at all...</div> </div> </div> </div>I can more so agree with this, contextually. While most are fine for most cases, there are a few relics, here and there, that could use some tuning, though not to the &quot;don't make sense at all...&quot; degree, unless you can site some examples that may not be coming to my mind at this particular moment. (Brawl and Do, for example, have never been sufficiently delineated enough for me relative to each other.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":1433,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","user":"Just John, Forever...","date":".  Thing made an emoticon when I didn't intend it to... plus spacing, as always... 08-18-2015, 01:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T12:39:30-07:00","id":631411,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just John, Forever...</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/619900-classic-world-of-darkness-iv-edition?p=631304#post631304\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Similarly, the distinction between Alertness and Awareness is a critical one,...</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is if this distinction is actually critical on a mechanical level enough to justify multiple Abilities.<br /> <br /> There's a fairly significant difference between fiber arts (knitting, weaving, etc.) and carpentry. But they're both under Crafts. Which isn't without some problems either, but it's a lot easier on the players to have, &quot;Crafts,&quot; and come up with ways to limit that broadness a bit, than forcing players to buy Crafts (X), Crafts (Y), Crafts (Z). Having lots of perception based Abilities is like having to buy lots of different instances of Crafts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now, as stated later on and in other threads, there is a validity to the argument that the two should be combined into Intuition, but then I think that conceptually overpowers the Ability: </div> </div> </div>Overpowers how?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> it would be similar to combining Medicine with Science (Biology/what-have-you) -- a doctor could be an excellent physician (Medicine:4) but only having functional biological knowledge outside of his applied practice vis-a-vis biological research/study (Biology:2); </div> </div> </div>I think this is a bad analogy. Medicine is, in many ways, separate from Science to accommodate characters that aren't mainstream medical professionals. Whether we're talking volunteer EMTs, or traditional medicine practitioners, it makes sense to have a significant separation between, &quot;I can heal people,&quot; and, &quot;I know how the human body works.&quot; Since you don't need to know most of a Bachelors in Biology to actually perform medical tasks.<br /> <br /> Now, any modern educated doctor should have some Science to go along with their Medicine (and Academics, and Law, and Computers, and Technology...), but some concepts are more demanding on such things than others.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> similarly, it would be like combining Computers with Technology/Repair -- there are plenty of highly competent (Computers:3+) programmers who only have functional knowledge of repairing/replacing the hardware involved (Tech:1 or 2), just as conversely there are plenty of engineers who may have Tech:4 but &quot;only&quot; have Computers:2 or (appropriate)Science: X = &lt;Tech). It's a similar concept here. </div> </div> </div>This seems a fundamental misunderstanding of how people think Computers should be removed.<br /> <br /> The problem with Computers is that it isn't the niche skill it was 25 years ago. Anyone born in 2000 or later, is growing up in a world where everything is a computer. Phones are computers. TVs are computers. Cars are computers. Watches are computers. And not just in a highly technical sense, in a, &quot;we use digital technology directly all the time.&quot;<br /> <br /> The idea of the &quot;hacker&quot; character that's just really good with computers is also extremely dated.<br /> <br /> Getting rid of Computers is a matter of changing the Ability list as times have changed. It's also not about shoving all of Computers into Technology. The physical aspects of Computers (assembly, repair, component invention) go into Technology like any other engineering field. The coding aspects go into Science.<br /> <br /> So the distinction between &quot;coder&quot; and &quot;technician&quot; remains solidly intact.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I can more so agree with this, contextually. While most are fine for most cases, there are a few relics, here and there, that could use some tuning, though not to the &quot;don't make sense at all...&quot; degree, unless you can site some examples that may not be coming to my mind at this particular moment. (Brawl and Do, for example, have never been sufficiently delineated enough for me relative to each other.) </div> </div> </div>Example:<br /> <br /> Alertness has no untrained penalty. So you can always just roll Perception at the base difficult to see if you notice something.<br /> <br /> Investigation can't be rolled without buying at least one dot. So, you can't look around a room to see if you spot anything in a systematic way (no matter how smart you are) unless you buy Investigation. You can only hope to spot something by happening to notice it (Alertness).<br /> <br /> Example:<br /> <br /> Lying has no untrained penalty (Subterfuge). Neither does trying to come up with thoughtful prose, and then deliver it (Expression)<br /> <br /> Acting does (Performance).<br /> <br /> More examples of the same line of comparison:<br /> <br /> Streetwise vs. Etiquette.<br /> <br /> Empathy vs. Animal Ken.<br /> <br /> Crafts or Drive vs. Technology (V20).<br /> <br /> --------<br /> <br /> Another way to look at it, if you don't like the &quot;untrained penalties&quot; thing but that includes grouping them by how &quot;intuitive&quot; they are supposed to be, is that there's no distinct grouping or theme to them.<br /> <br /> It's not like the nWOD that just does Mental/Social/Physical. Or Exalted that groups them by thematic roles (Dawns are warriors, so fighty stuff goes there. Twilights are the brains, so the being smart stuff goes there, etc.)<br /> <br /> While some concepts fall into obvious picks for primary category as-is (generally going to be Mental/Knowledge focused ones though), plenty of concepts clearly need to cross those lines to make any sense.<br /> <br /> Example:<br /> <br /> A medical doctor can stick pretty neatly into the Knowledges section as is, with Talents and Skills being flavor. Some specialties might imply ratings in a few things, but nothing core to being a good healer.<br /> <br /> A police detective has to be all over the map. Talents are important (Alertness, Brawl, Empathy/Subterfuge, Intimidation, Streetwise), Skills are important (Drive, Melee, Firearms, Stealth), and Knowledges are important (Academics, Computers, Investigation, Law, Technology). And that's not including any sort of specialty (K-9 needing Animal Ken, undercover needing Performance, etc.).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T14:11:20-07:00","id":631540,"title":"","content":"First I was thinking my answer to <b>Just John</b>, them I saw this:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just John, Forever...</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">the DA/Ravenloft hybrid I've been running again this summer for some friends</div> </div> </div> </div>That kept me hypnotized for quite some time...<br /> <br /> But, well, <b>Heavy Arms</b> already explained.<br /> <br /> We could have lots of Sciences, lots of Crafts, lots of a huge variety of things. To account the extreme variation on professionals you find IRL, you would have to make an equally huge variety of skills. That's what a simulator do, and WoD got stuck in an awkward middle ground for simulation, with Crafts accounting for an insane variety of skill sets (each of them complex itself, mind you) while knowing a culture (Academics or a Secondary Skill) is differentiated from knowing how to behave yourself in said culture (Etiquette, one of the silliest skills on the spread).<br /> <br /> So the question isn't if Alertness and Awareness are distinct things (they are), but if they need to be mechanically distinguished as standard(they do?). A good base system would have a simple set of basic skills equally usable on most chronicles, at least as far as possible, without dead or semi-dead weight, or skills so important that you take them anyway, but wonder why the game just forced you to buy this and pretended not to. And them modular subsystems like Specialties, Expertise, or maybe a better rule on Secondary Abilities (renamed to Optional Abilities) to help give it the tweaks you need for your particular chronicle (if you need to distinguish heavily Alertness and Awareness).<br /> <br /> And in the Ability Categories what's dated isn't the current distribution. Is the very dumb idea of distinguish categories as Talents, Skills and Knowledges. This doesn't make sense from the POV of how the human mind work and learn, it doesn't make sense in the POV of how literary character concepts work, except <u>very</u> specific concepts, and from a game design POV it is simply ludicrous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T18:39:58-07:00","id":631814,"title":"","content":"I think dividing Abilities into Physical, Mental and Social makes way more sense. But I never cared for prioritising traits anyway (I always let characters spend their Attribute and Ability points in whichever of the three categories they like). You can simply make unskilled penalties an ST decision based on the complexity or technical proficiency required for a given action (someone without Medicine isn't going to be able to do brain surgery, but they might be able to improvise a dressing at a higher than normal difficulty).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-19T00:38:23-07:00","id":632044,"title":"","content":"As far as I can tell, they could be divided in Blossom, Bubbles and Buttercup, and we would have a better categorization. And the unskilled penalties are arbitrary anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}